---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { dpo, links } from "../data/site";

const version = "2.7";
const updated = "26 November 2026";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What Pesa Clarity keeps on your phone, what its server holds, and your rights."
>
  <article class="container privacy-page">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="muted">
        Version {version}, last updated {updated}.
      </p>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        Pesa Clarity works out your budget on your phone. Your transactions,
        messages, statements and the questions you ask about them stay on it.
        Our server does five narrow jobs and never sees a transaction. If you
        turn on Firefly III sync, your transactions are sent from your phone to
        the Firefly III server you choose, and never to ours.
      </p>

      <h2>What stays on your phone</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Messages from the banks and wallets you choose. Messages from every
          other sender are ignored and never stored.
        </li>
        <li>
          On iPhone, messages come from a Shortcuts automation you set up. Each
          one waits in a file only Pesa Clarity can open until the app reads it,
          for at most 35 days.
        </li>
        <li>
          The fields read from those messages: amount, date, payee, balance and
          fee. Once a message is read, its text is deleted.
        </li>
        <li>
          Messages the app is still learning. They wait in Messages to check
          until you check them, then their text is deleted. The suggestions come
          from a reader that runs on your phone.
        </li>
        <li>
          Layouts you save with "Read messages like this the same way". A layout
          keeps the message's wording with the amount, payee, date and other
          details taken out, and numbers reduced to their shape, such as
          9,999.99.
        </li>
        <li>
          Statement PDFs. Their passwords are used once, in memory, then
          discarded. PDFs are never uploaded.
        </li>
        <li>Budgets, debts, goals, payees, categories and every insight.</li>
        <li>
          In a business space: customers' and suppliers' names and what they
          owe, the phone numbers you add or pick from your contacts (the app
          gets only the contact you pick), and the pictures and documents kept
          for receipts and invoices, such as your logo, your M-PESA QR code and
          receipts you attach.
        </li>
        <li>
          Receipts, invoices and reports are made on your phone. They leave it
          only when you share or print them, in the app or on the printer you
          choose. A Bluetooth receipt printer you pair gets only what you print.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        All of this sits in an encrypted database. The key is held by your
        phone's secure hardware. With app lock on, it is released only after you
        unlock the app with your PIN, fingerprint or Face ID. Without app lock,
        it is released when the app opens.
      </p>

      <h2>Ask</h2>
      <p>
        Ask answers questions about your money in your own words, such as "how
        much did I spend on food last month?". Small models that ship inside the
        app read your question and word the answer, on your phone. Every number
        comes from your own records in the space you have open.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Nothing about your money is sent anywhere. Your questions, the answers
          and the records they come from never leave your phone, and Ask works
          without internet.
        </li>
        <li>
          Ask has no federated learning. "Improve the app" never includes
          anything from Ask.
        </li>
        <li>
          Conversations are kept in the space's encrypted database for as long
          as you choose in Ask settings: until you delete them, or for a year,
          90, 30 or 7 days. You can also switch saving off.
        </li>
        <li>
          Words you teach Ask, such as "chakula" for Food, stay in that space
          too.
        </li>
        <li>
          If you choose, Ask downloads a small money model (Ask settings,
          Answers). The file comes from GitHub (Microsoft), where the app is
          published, so GitHub sees that one download request, with your IP
          address as for any website visit. No questions, answers, records or
          other data of yours are sent. The model then runs on your phone only,
          is kept out of backups, and you can delete it any time.
        </li>
        <li>
          You can delete one conversation, or every conversation in a space.
          Backups leave conversations out.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What our server holds</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>Sign-in</dt>
        <dd>
          Your account id, the email or Google account you sign in with, and
          when you signed up, in and out. Our server stores your email or Google
          account only as a keyed hash, never the address itself. One-time
          sign-in codes are deleted within a day of expiring, and sign-in
          records after 90 days. You can also use the app without an account; it
          then contacts our server only for the update check and any crash
          report you choose to send.
        </dd>
        <dt>Encrypted backups</dt>
        <dd>
          If you choose our vault, backups are encrypted on your phone with your
          passphrase before upload. We hold only the encrypted file and cannot
          read it. We keep your latest 10 backups and delete older ones. The
          server checks with Google (Android) or Apple (iPhone) that each backup
          comes from the genuine app; neither sees the backup. You can back up
          to your own Google Drive, your phone, an SD card or Files on iPhone
          instead.
        </dd>
        <dt>Model updates</dt>
        <dd>
          The app does not send any training updates today. With "Help improve
          the app" on, it only learns from the categories you confirm, on your
          phone, to make its own suggestions better; nothing about it leaves the
          phone. If we ever add sending updates to improve the shared model, it
          will be opt-in, and we will update this policy before it starts.
        </dd>
        <dt>Update check</dt>
        <dd>
          Once a day, with or without an account, the app asks our server
          whether a newer version or a notice is waiting, such as a fix for a
          bank that changed its messages. The request carries the app's version,
          which build it is (Play, F-Droid, iPhone or website) and the platform,
          and, unless you switch it off, the counting number described below. It
          carries no account, device or phone id. The answer is signed, so the
          app shows only notices that really come from us. The app asks you to
          update before you carry on only when a version is known to get amounts
          wrong.
        </dd>
        <dt>Monthly count</dt>
        <dd>
          So we know how many phones use Pesa Clarity, the update check carries
          a random number that your phone makes each month and replaces on the
          1st of the next. It is not made from anything about you or your phone,
          and it is never sent with anything else. Our server uses it only to
          count each phone once a day and once a month, adding one to totals by
          app version, build and, for the month, country. It keeps those totals,
          which name no one. It keeps a scrambled copy of the number only until
          the day or month is over, then deletes it. We work out the country
          from your internet address while answering, with a database kept on
          our server (<a href="https://db-ip.com">IP Geolocation by DB-IP</a>),
          and never store or log the address. We never look at a total under 10.
          To stop, switch off "Count this phone in monthly totals" in Privacy;
          the update check carries on without the number. In the F-Droid build
          it is off until you switch it on, and the update check itself runs
          only after you say yes to it.
        </dd>
        <dt>Crash reports</dt>
        <dd>
          If the app closes because of a fault, it asks on the next start
          whether to send us a crash report, and shows you exactly what it would
          send. A report holds the app's version and build, the phone's system
          version (such as Android 13) and chip type (such as arm64), the kind
          of error, and where in the app's own code it happened, as numbers. It
          never holds amounts, messages, names, phone numbers, contacts or
          anything you typed, and it carries no account, device or install id.
          It is not linked to the monthly count. We keep reports for 90 days,
          then only how many there were and when. In Privacy you can choose Ask
          each time, Always send or Never send.
        </dd>
      </dl>

      <h2>Who runs our server</h2>
      <p>
        These services run it for us and process data only on our instructions:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Google Cloud runs the server. Like any web host, it keeps standard
          request logs, such as IP address and time, for security. It keeps no
          request logs for the update check or crash reports, and its other logs
          never include the counting number or crash reports' contents.
        </li>
        <li>Neon stores sign-in data in its database.</li>
        <li>Cloudflare R2 stores encrypted backups.</li>
        <li>Brevo sends sign-in codes by email.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you ask us to email you a copy of this policy, Brevo sends it to the
        address you give. We use that address once and do not keep it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some of them store data outside Kenya. We use them under their data
        processing terms, as the Kenya Data Protection Act 2019 requires for
        transfers abroad.
      </p>

      <h2>Shared spaces</h2>
      <p>
        A Household or a chama/savings group space can be shared with other
        people. Members share that space's budget, transactions, goals and
        member list. Your personal space is never included.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          You share by sending an encrypted file (.pcshare) through any app you
          choose, such as WhatsApp, email or Bluetooth. Our server is not
          involved.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each file is end-to-end encrypted with AES-256-GCM, using a key for
          that space and each member's X25519 key. The app that carries the
          file, and Pesa Clarity, cannot read it.
        </li>
        <li>
          When you join, your phone makes its own key. The private key never
          leaves your phone. A join request holds your name and public key, and
          safety words let you both check it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Approvals and declines are signed on the approver's phone. A record of
          who changed or approved what is kept in the space's history on
          members' phones.
        </li>
        <li>
          The camera is used only to scan join and invite QR codes. The images
          are not stored or sent.
        </li>
        <li>
          Members you share with can see the shared space's data and keep
          copies. Leaving or removing someone stops future updates, but cannot
          erase copies already on their phones.
        </li>
        <li>
          Shared data stays on members' phones, encrypted. Deleting a space
          deletes it from your phone.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Firefly III</h2>
      <p>
        Firefly III is a free personal finance manager that people run on their
        own server. Pesa Clarity can send your transactions to it, so you do not
        have to type them in again.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          It is off until you turn it on in Profile. You choose the server,
          which spaces to send and from which date.
        </li>
        <li>
          For each transaction the app sends the amount, date, payee, category,
          account names and notes, such as the{" "}
          <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span> code. It also sends each account's
          balance on the start date and the space's name as a tag. It never
          sends message text.
        </li>
        <li>
          It sends them from your phone straight to the server address you type.
          Nothing goes through our server, and we never see what is sent.
        </li>
        <li>
          Changes and deletes in the app are sent too. Nothing is read back from
          your server.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your Firefly III token stays in your phone's secure storage. It is
          sent only to your server, and never put in a backup, a log or a crash
          report.
        </li>
        <li>
          Turning it off stops sending and removes the token from your phone.
          What was already sent stays on your server. Deleting a space stops
          sending it; what was sent stays too.
        </li>
        <li>
          We cannot see or delete anything on your server. You look after it and
          who can reach it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Exchange rates</h2>
      <p>
        To count money in other currencies, the app downloads today's exchange
        rates from a public rates service. The request carries only your base
        currency's code, such as KES; the service sees your IP address, as any
        website does. You can set your own rates instead.
      </p>

      <h2>Giving (F-Droid build)</h2>
      <p>
        The F-Droid build has a Support screen for anyone who wants to give a
        small gift by <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span>. Giving is your choice
        and never unlocks anything.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The Support screen opens CitaPay's page in your browser. Its address
          carries only the amount you chose.
        </li>
        <li>
          CitaPay, run by Citatech, and Safaricom's{" "}
          <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span> process the payment. They see what
          any <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span> payment shows, such as your
          phone number and name, plus the amount and any note you type.
        </li>
        <li>
          CitaPay's terms cover the payment:{" "}
          <a href={links.citapayTerms}>pay.citatech.cloud/legal/terms</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Pesa Clarity receives only the money and the{" "}
          <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span> notice of it.
        </li>
        <li>
          The app sends nothing to CitaPay. To say thank you, it recognises your
          gift from your own <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span> message, on your
          phone.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Your choices</h2>
      <p>The app asks on first run, and you can change these any time:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Help improve the app: on by default. Today it only lets the app learn
          from your confirmed categories on your phone; nothing is sent.
        </li>
        <li>Contribute to licensed models: off unless you opt in.</li>
        <li>
          Count this phone in monthly totals: on by default; off by default in
          the F-Droid build.
        </li>
        <li>
          Crash reports: the app asks each time, unless you choose Always send
          or Never send.
        </li>
        <li>Firefly III sync: off unless you turn it on.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Pesa Clarity has no ads and no third-party trackers, and no analytics
        beyond the monthly count above.
      </p>

      <h2>Keeping it safe</h2>
      <p>
        Data on your phone is encrypted, and everything sent to our server
        travels over an encrypted connection. Firefly III sync uses an encrypted
        connection too, unless you choose a plain one to a server on your home
        network. Deleting your account removes your account, sign-in records and
        vault backups from our server.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the Kenya Data Protection Act 2019 you can ask to see, correct or
        delete the data we hold about you, take a copy with you, and object to
        how it is used or to automated decisions. To ask, write to our Data
        Protection Officer, {dpo.name}, at{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${dpo.email}`}>{dpo.email}</a>. We will answer within
        30 days. You can delete your account from inside the app. If you are
        unhappy with our answer, you can complain to the Office of the Data
        Protection Commissioner (<a href="https://www.odpc.go.ke">odpc.go.ke</a>
        ).
      </p>
      <p>Pesa Clarity is for people aged 18 and over.</p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site sets no cookies, runs no analytics and loads nothing from
        other servers. Fonts are served from this site. If you choose a light or
        dark theme, your browser keeps that choice on your device; it is never
        sent to us. The site runs two small scripts: one switches the theme, and
        one searches the site in your browser. Nothing you type is sent
        anywhere. The web host may keep standard request logs, such as IP
        address and time, for security.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        When this policy changes, the app shows what changed and asks you to
        read it before you carry on.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Write to our Data Protection Officer, {dpo.name}, at{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${dpo.email}`}>{dpo.email}</a>. You can also ask in
        public on GitHub (<a href={links.github}>github.com/pesaclarity</a>), or
        report a security issue privately using the security policy there.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .privacy-page {
    padding-block: var(--space-7) var(--space-8);
  }

  h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
